During sleep, the brain forms and strengthens neural connections, which are crucial for memory consolidation.
Research indicates that sleep deprivation leads to reduced brain activity in areas responsible for learning and memory, such as the hippocampus.
Analogous to how a computer needs downtime to process data, the brain requires sleep to organize and store information effectively.
Students who get adequate sleep perform better on memory and learning tasks compared to those who are sleep-deprived.
Long-term sleep deprivation can lead to cognitive decline, making it harder to learn and retain information over time.